\statement{A.77}{Proved}{One finite relation module detects the prescribed-frame obstruction that every scalar iteration misses}
Let \(\Pi=C_2\amalg C_3=\langle s,r\rangle\), let
\(q:\Pi\twoheadrightarrow Q\) be a finite quotient injecting both
factors, and put \(L=\ker q\). Define
\[
 \Phi_p(L)=\overline{[L,L]L^p},\qquad
 G_p=\Pi/\Phi_p(L)\quad(p=2,3),\qquad
 G_6=\Pi/\overline{[L,L]L^6}.
 \tag{A.77.1}
\]
Then
\[
 \operatorname{im}\bigl(C_{G_2}(s)\longrightarrow Q\bigr)
       =\langle s_Q\rangle,
 \qquad
 \operatorname{im}\bigl(C_{G_3}(r)\longrightarrow Q\bigr)
       =\langle r_Q\rangle.
 \tag{A.77.2}
\]
Both equalities hold with \(G_6\) in place of the corresponding
\(G_p\). If \(Q\) retains the standard \(S_3\) quotient, an
automorphism of \(G_6\) preserving the kernel of
\(G_6\to Q\) therefore determines uniquely the
\(Q\)-images of its normalized conjugators of \(s\) and
\(r^{-1}\), whenever these conjugators exist: the normalizations
are images \(1\) and \(s\), respectively, in \(S_3\).
If the automorphism is involutory and acts trivially on \(S_3\),
both lower normalizer norms equal one.

For the original datum of A.76, let \(E\) be its actual finite
six-cup quotient (A.76.7). Every full normalized positive lift
retaining the original finite action and s-frame has negative
square nontrivial already on
\[
 \mathcal G=\Pi/\Phi_2\bigl(\ker(\Pi\to E)\bigr).
 \tag{A.77.3}
\]
The orders of these specified finite groups are
\[
 |E|=2^{1620}\cdot72\cdot7^{13},\qquad
 |\mathcal G|=|E|\,2^{1+|E|/6}.
 \tag{A.77.4}
\]
Nevertheless, all iterated actual scalar refinements of the old
finite datum admit the original compatible involution and both
norm-one frames.

\begin{proof}
\emph{The first relative module controls the centralizer image.}\par
For \(p=2,3\), write \(a=s,r\), respectively. Let \(m\in\Pi\)
lift an element of \(C_{G_p}(a)\), and set
\[
                       J=q^{-1}(\langle a_Q\rangle).
\]
The two elements \(a\) and \(mam^{-1}\) lie in \(J\), although
\(m\) need not lie in \(J\): their images in \(Q\) are equal.
They coincide modulo \(\Phi_p(L)\), which is contained in
\(\Phi_p(J)\), and consequently have the same image in the
mod-\(p\) abelianization of \(J\).

The open profinite Kurosh theorem writes \(J\) as a finite free
profinite product of copies of \(C_p\) and a finite-rank full
free profinite group. There are no factors from the other source
cyclic group, since \(q\) injects it and \(J/L\) has order \(p\).
The free-product torsion theorem conjugates each element of order
\(p\) into one of the cyclic factors. Different factors and
different nonidentity powers of a fixed factor generator have
different images in the mod-\(p\) abelianization. Hence
\(a\) and \(mam^{-1}\) are conjugate in \(J\). These are the
open-subgroup and torsion statements in the full profinite
category; see \citet{A_HR1985}.

Choose \(j\in J\) with \(j^{-1}mam^{-1}j=a\). The full
free-factor centralizer is \(C_\Pi(a)=\langle a\rangle\), so
\(j^{-1}m\in\langle a\rangle\). Thus
\(q(m)\in\langle a_Q\rangle\). The reverse inclusion is
immediate. Since \(G_6\) maps to both \(G_p\), (A.77.2) also
holds for \(G_6\).

The kernel \(L\) is free profinite of rank \(d=1+|Q|/6\), by
open Kurosh and the Euler characteristic of \(C_2\amalg C_3\).
Consequently
\[
            |G_p|=|Q|p^d,\qquad |G_6|=|Q|6^d.
 \tag{A.77.5}
\]
This specifies finite quotients directly; no enumeration of their
elements is required.

\emph{Extracting the lower frames.}\par
Suppose \(b\in\operatorname{Aut}(G_6)\) and
\[
          b(s)=\beta s\beta^{-1},\qquad
          b(r)=Jr^{-1}J^{-1}.
 \tag{A.77.6}
\]
Two conjugators for either equation differ on the right by the
corresponding centralizer. Equation (A.77.2) bounds their
\(Q\)-ambiguity by the relevant cyclic source factor. If their
\(S_3\)-images agree, that ambiguity is trivial. This proves the
asserted uniqueness. Whenever an induced action on \(Q\) is used,
stability of the kernel of \(G_6\to Q\) is retained explicitly.

If \(b^2=1\), then \(b(\beta)\beta\) centralizes \(s\) and
\(b(J)J\) centralizes \(r\). Their \(S_3\)-images are one under
the stated normalizations and trivial \(S_3\)-action. Their
\(Q\)-images therefore lie in the intersection of the pure
\(S_3\)-kernel with the corresponding cyclic factor, and equal
one. This is a necessary lifting condition; existence of such an
action on \(G_6\) is not asserted.

\emph{A single actual noncentral detector for A.76.}\par
A.76 proves that every full candidate retaining its data preserves
\(E\) and has the prescribed action on its six coefficient copies.
Canonical \(c\) preserves the same kernel. Its characteristic
subgroup \(\Phi_2(\ker(\Pi\to E))\) is therefore preserved by
both, so the negative companion \(b=hc\) acts on the same finite
\(\mathcal G\) for every candidate.

Let \(\beta\) be the candidate's full s-conjugator retaining the
original finite frame, and put
\[
                         n=\beta^{-1}b(\beta^{-1}).
 \tag{A.77.7}
\]
Before \(b^2=1\) has been established, this notation denotes a
group element, not a square in a semidirect product by \(C_2\).
Its image in \(E\) is a normalizer square because the induced
negative action on \(E\) is involutory. That image lies in the
six-cup kernel and is nonidentity by the exhaustive invariant
(A.76.10). It therefore lies outside \(\langle s_E\rangle\).
Equation (A.77.2), now with \(Q=E\), shows that \(n\) cannot
centralize \(s\) in \(\mathcal G\). But direct substitution in
\(b(s)=\beta s\beta^{-1}\) gives
\[
                           b^2(s)=n^{-1}sn.
 \tag{A.77.8}
\]
Thus \(b_{\mathcal G}^2(s_{\mathcal G})\ne s_{\mathcal G}\).
The six attained coefficient copies add \(6\cdot108=648\)
binary dimensions to A.76's old finite quotient. This proves
(A.77.4) and the claimed finite detection.

\emph{Every scalar iteration still passes.}\par
Put \(N_0=\ker(\Pi\to D_0)\), for A.76's old quotient, and
\(N_{j+1}=\overline{[N_j,\Pi]}\). The original A.56 kernel
satisfies \(U=[U,\Pi]\) and \(U\subseteq N_0\). Inductively,
\[
                            U\subseteq N_j\quad(j\ge0).
 \tag{A.77.9}
\]
Hence every \(\Pi/N_j\) is an actual quotient of the same
\(D=\Pi/U\). Its original negative involution and both
norm-one frames descend compatibly to all these quotients.
The subgroups are stable because \(N_0\) is stable and the
operation \(N\mapsto[N,\Pi]\) is equivariant.

Each quotient is finite. Inductively, if \(m=[\Pi:N_j]\), the
subgroup \(Z=N_j/[N_j,\Pi]\) is central of index \(m\) in
\(\Pi/[N_j,\Pi]\). It is finitely generated. Transfer restricted
to \(Z\) is the \(m\)-th power map, and it factors through the
abelianization of the ambient group, a quotient of
\(\Pi^{\rm ab}=C_6\). Thus \(Z\) has exponent dividing \(6m\)
and is finite. The existing full positive lift realizes all these
scalar data simultaneously. Its prescribed-frame negative square
is nevertheless detected by (A.77.3). Arbitrarily many scalar
iterations therefore do not replace the noncentral lifting gate.
\end{proof}

The successful reselections below and in A.64--A.75 remain
compatible with this result. The finite detector retains A.76's
literal cusp and prescribed finite frame. Neither an unframed
nonexistence theorem nor a full peripheral conjugacy is inferred.
